<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lambert I, Count of Louvain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 950–1015</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Lambert the Bearded · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginarids">Reginarids</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lambert I (c. 950–1015), called the Bearded, was the first Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a>, a younger son of the Reginarid count Reginar III of Hainaut. From around 1003 he built up lands around Leuven and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, laying the foundations of the later Duchy of Brabant. He was killed at the Battle of Florennes in 1015 fighting Duke Godfrey II of Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lambert belonged to the Reginars, a Lotharingian noble family. His great-uncle Gilbert had ruled Lotharingia as duke and was killed in 939 after rebelling against the German Ottonians. His father, Reginar III, was exiled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in about 958 after another rebellion and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 973. Soon afterwards Lambert and his brother Reginar IV invaded Hainaut, killing Count Werner and his brother Rainold, who held their father's county, until Emperor Otto II besieged them, destroyed their castle and banished them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The brothers withdrew to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and won the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-duke-of-lower-lorraine">Charles, Duke of Lower Lorraine</a>, a brother of King Lothair of France. In 976 they invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> with Charles. Lambert married Charles's daughter Gerberga, and Reginar IV a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a>. In 978 they fought for France against Otto II. Before 995 Lambert was fighting in the Pagus of Brabant against Count Ansfried, and Alpertus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> called him a leader of bandits who hid in forests and swamps. The basis of his claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> is unclear; it may have been granted by his father-in-law.</p><p>He was first described as count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> in 1003. In 1006 he allied with the Counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> as they gained a foothold east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, and Emperor Henry II held his son hostage. In 1013, with his nephew Reginar V and Robert II, Count of Namur, he defeated Bishop Balderic II of Liège at Hoegaarden, a step in his family's long push into the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesbaye">Hesbaye</a> east of Leuven.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was killed at Florennes on 12 September 1015. Dietmar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> called him the worst person in his whole country, who had strangled many people in churches with bell ropes, though imperial politics, among other things, shaped how the chronicles depicted him. The marriage of Reginar V to a daughter of Count Herman later reconciled the two hostile families. Lambert was the patrilineal ancestor of all later counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> and dukes of Brabant until John III, Duke of Brabant, who died in 1355.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lambert I of Louvain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1003 <b>Lambert I the Bearded</b>, a member of the Reginarid family, took the title of count of Louvain.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-louvain">County of Louvain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in Florennes, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.88,4.7&amp;year=992" aria-label="Leuven on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lambert I, Count of Louvain"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5254 5768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5254 5768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.88,4.7&amp;year=992">What was here in 992 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
